WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
Typical iOS applications execute AOT compiled Swift almost instantly.

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.200
React native applications operate differently.

3
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:15.100
They face a startup penalty because a secondary infrastructure must initialize before any business logic can run.

4
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.400
Before your logic executes, the system must boot a JavaScript engine,

5
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.900
load a bundle from disk, and drive an initial render.

6
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.600
We can visualize this process as a five-phase sequential pipeline

7
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:31.300
that dictates exactly how long it takes for a user to see the first screen.

8
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:36.100
In unoptimized builds, critical milliseconds bleed out across these specific boundaries,

9
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.600
delaying the final interactive state.

10
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.700
Our goal is to map these execution boundaries, utilize the architecture of the Hermes engine,

11
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:47.700
and apply deferral techniques to compress this entire timeline.

12
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.700
Understanding this sequential pipeline allows infrastructure engineers to target the specific delays

13
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:56.600
that prevent React native from achieving native-level startup performance.

14
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.100
Phase 1 is native initialization.

15
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:06.400
This begins the moment the process launches, triggering pure objective C and Swift execution in the app delegate.

16
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.000
During this phase, the system allocates the JSI runtime,

17
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.800
initializes the fabric renderer, and prepares the turbo-module registry.

18
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.300
Because this is strictly native execution,

19
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:21.400
it typically completes in single-digit milliseconds on modern devices.

20
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.000
It is rarely the source of your bottleneck.

21
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.700
Phase 2 is engine initialization.

22
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.700
This is where the Hermes engine instance is created and prepares its heap for execution.

23
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.000
Hermes is designed as an embedded engine with a very small start-up footprint,

24
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
allowing it to complete this initialization in under five milliseconds.

25
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Since the native and engine boot sequences are functionally instantaneous,

26
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.700
optimization efforts should be directed at the subsequent JavaScript ingestion and evaluation phases.

27
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:55.100
In phase 3, the engine must load the primary JavaScript bundle from the app bundle on desk.

28
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.800
Legacy engines forced a heavy cost.

29
00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.800
They had to read raw source text, parse it,

30
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.700
and construct an abstract syntax tree before code could run.

31
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:10.100
This parsing blocks the main thread, adding up to 400 milliseconds to your startup time.

32
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.800
Hermes uses a head-of-time compilation.

33
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:19.300
During the Xcode build, the compiler processes your JavaScript and outputs an HBC binary file.

34
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.400
This shifts the execution model.

35
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:27.200
Hermes uses OS-level MLP demand-paging to map this binary file directly to memory.

36
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.100
Because of this memory mapping, large bundles don't require a full upfront read.

37
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:37.400
The operating system only loads specific pages when the engine actually executes that code path.

38
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.900
This makes legacy RAM bundles obsolete.

39
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.400
Enabling them alongside Hermes provides no performance benefit

40
00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:47.200
and can actually conflict with the engine's internal bytecode compilation.

41
00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.800
To confirm your app is using this binary format,

42
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.100
check your Xcode build logs for the emit binary flag during the bundle phase.

43
00:02:54.100 --> 00:03:00.800
Moving from source text parsing to binary memory mapping changes how we think about optimization.

44
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:06.100
The bottleneck is no longer how the code is parsed, but when it is first executed.

45
00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.900
Phase 4 is JS evaluation.

46
00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:15.300
This begins when the entry module executes and resolves every synchronous requirement in your dependency tree.

47
00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:20.700
The Metro Bundler uses common JS semantics, making static tree shaking difficult.

48
00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.400
By default, almost everything in your bundle lights up and executes it once.

49
00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.200
Because we cannot easily delete unused code,

50
00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:32.100
we must use aggressive deferral to keep this phase from expanding.

51
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:39.000
Enabling inline requires in your Metro config allows the Bundler to rewrite top-level imports to the call site.

52
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.700
This ensures a module only executes when the function using it is actually called.

53
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.600
But use caution with modules that have side effects at the point of requirement,

54
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.700
like certain analytics SDKs.

55
00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:54.100
Deferring them can lead to unexpected behavior if they initialize too late.

56
00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:58.900
You can further optimize by wrapping non-critical screens in React.lazy,

57
00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:03.900
which defers the creation of the shadow tree until the user navigates to that screen.

58
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.900
Since Metro struggles to prune unused code,

59
00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:10.900
structurally deferring execution until after the app has started

60
00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.100
is the most effective way to protect phase 4.

61
00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:18.000
Finally, in phase 5, React reconciles the component tree,

62
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.300
and the fabric renderer commits the layout to the screen.

63
00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:24.200
The main bottleneck here is often non-technical.

64
00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:28.800
It's the time spent decoding and rendering images needed for the first frame.

65
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:34.800
Converting assets to the WebP format can reduce these file sizes by 25 to 35 percent,

66
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.600
speeding up the initial transfer and decode.

67
00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.300
However, using a standard JavaScript require for a splash image

68
00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:46.200
means the process cannot start until the JS runtime is ready to resolve that path.

69
00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.100
For graphics used on the launch path,

70
00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:51.500
you should register them in Xcode's asset catalog instead.

71
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:56.700
This allows the OS to decode the image while the JavaScript engine is still booting.

72
00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.700
These assets are handled by the native image cache

73
00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:05.000
and are available for display before the JavaScript runtime has even finished initializing.

74
00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:09.400
Moving these heavy first frame assets into the native OS layers

75
00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:13.500
removes them from the React native startup bottleneck entirely.

76
00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:17.700
These architectural changes are only effective if you can measure their impact.

77
00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:20.800
Every optimization must be backed by data.

78
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:26.200
You should never use the iOS simulator or console.time to measure startup performance.

79
00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.600
The hostmax CPU is too powerful,

80
00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:32.600
providing metrics that don't reflect real-world device performance.

81
00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:35.400
Begin with a source map explorer audit.

82
00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:39.200
This helps identify large transitive dependencies that are bloating your bundle

83
00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.400
before they ever reach the device.

84
00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.100
For the most accurate measurements,

85
00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:46.400
use Xcode instruments with the OS sigpost tool.

86
00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:52.000
This provides a clearer timeline of the intervals spent in each phase on physical hardware.

87
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.100
In a release build,

88
00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:58.900
React native automatically emits signposts for the five execution phases we've discussed.

89
00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:02.800
You can also inject custom native signposts through a turbo module

90
00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.400
to measure the specific performance of your own business logic.

91
00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:10.200
By combining these measurements with the Hermes bytecode architecture

92
00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.100
and strategic code deferral,

93
00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:15.700
you can systematically remove the React native startup penalty

94
00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:18.700
and reach native performance levels.

